
# JotDrop

**Get it out of your head. Get it into your calendar.**

> **Status: Currently in Development**
>
> JotDrop is a working prototype that I currently use for personal productivity. The application functions as intended for my own Google accounts, but it isn't quite ready for public use.
>
> Setting it up for another Google account currently requires additional technical steps, including creating and importing Google Apps Script code, configuring authentication IDs, and granting the necessary permissions.
>
> I'm continuing development with the goal of making JotDrop accessible to others without requiring them to go through these steps.

## Why I Built JotDrop

Recently, I've found myself texting reminders to myself or even a friend just so I don't forget things I need to do.

I usually separate everything by line, something like:

```text
Fill out applications
Complete new project and document it for project portfolio
Dance like crazy
```

One morning, I remember thinking:

*"I wish I could just copy and paste these messages directly into Google Calendar and Tasks to save myself some time."*

I've always found organizing calendars and to-do lists tedious. I don't particularly enjoy spending time opening Google Calendar, creating an event, choosing a date, saving it, switching to Google Tasks, and repeating the process for everything else on my mind.

But as someone with ADHD, keeping track of everything without writing it down is incredibly difficult for me.

I need to get my thoughts out of my head and somewhere I can reliably reference them later. The problem is that actually organizing those thoughts into a calendar or task management system takes time and effort.

Sometimes I'll have several things I want to accomplish, but the process of organizing them becomes another task in itself.

That's what led me to create **JotDrop**.

I wanted something that worked with how I already write reminders, without requiring me to completely change my habits.

## What JotDrop Does

JotDrop is a lightweight web application that takes unstructured notes and turns them into organized entries for Google Calendar and Google Tasks.

Instead of manually creating each calendar event or task, I can write everything down in one place, let the application separate the entries, review them, and upload everything with a single button.

### Quick Notes and Automatic Organization

One of my favorite features is how easily I can enter multiple tasks.

I personally prefer separating each thought by line, just like I would when texting someone a list of reminders.

JotDrop can process these notes, distinguish individual entries, and organize them for review.

I can also include additional information or notes without needing to manually create a separate entry for everything.

The goal is to make entering tasks as natural as writing down whatever comes to mind.

### Google Calendar and Google Tasks Integration

Once JotDrop has organized the entries, I can review them and add them to Google Calendar and Google Tasks in one workflow.

I also implemented an option to **duplicate Google Calendar events as Google Tasks**.

For example, if I schedule time to work on a project, I can have that event appear on my calendar while also creating a corresponding task that I can check off when it's completed.

This helps me keep track of both what I've scheduled and what I've actually accomplished.

### Customizable UI Themes and Priority Flags

I wanted JotDrop to feel personal, so I spent time building a customizable interface.

The application includes:

- Customizable UI themes with different color combinations.
- Color-coded priority flags to visually distinguish important entries.
- Appearance customization to suit individual preferences.
- An interface designed to make organizing tasks and events more comfortable.

Everyone organizes information differently, and I wanted to give users some freedom in how their workspace looks and feels.

## How I Built It

I developed JotDrop as a web-based application with a lightweight frontend for entering, reviewing, and organizing notes.

For the backend, I created a **Google Apps Script** that handles the integration with Google Calendar and Google Tasks.

The script allows JotDrop to communicate with Google's services and create the corresponding entries.

I built the application around my own productivity habits and continued refining the interface as I found things that could make the process easier.

One of my main priorities was reducing the number of individual steps required to organize everything.

## Current Development Status

JotDrop currently works as intended for my personal use.

However, it's still a prototype, and getting it working with additional Google accounts requires manual configuration of Google Apps Script, authentication credentials, and account permissions.

I want to refine that process before making the application publicly available.

For now, this repository serves primarily as documentation of my personal project and its continued development.

## Future Plans

My immediate goal is to finish refining the Google integration and simplify the process of connecting additional accounts.

Once the Google version is polished, I'd also like to expand JotDrop into **Microsoft environments**, potentially supporting Microsoft Outlook Calendar and Microsoft To Do.

I'd eventually like JotDrop to become something anyone can use to quickly turn their thoughts into organized tasks and calendar entries, regardless of which ecosystem they prefer.

## Final Thoughts

JotDrop started because I was tired of spending more time organizing my to-do lists than I felt was necessary.

I already had a habit of writing everything down. I just wanted a faster way to turn those notes into something useful.

It's a relatively simple idea, but solving a problem I personally deal with has made this one of my more meaningful projects.

I'm continuing to develop it as I discover new ways to improve the workflow.